    "etymology": {
     "text": "A contraction of ع ن ن or ع ي ن (in the sense of viewing) and يَدَا (“hand”), i.e. “at hand, before one’s eyes”; compare لَدَى and لَدُنْ.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "عِنْدَمَا (“when”) is simply عِنْدَ + مَا."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "عِنْدَ is a preposition, not a verb: it does not change for tense. “I had” = كَانَ عِنْدِي; “I don’t have” = لَيْسَ عِنْدِي.",
     "The thing you have is the grammatical subject, so it is nominative and usually indefinite: عِنْدِي جَزَرَةٌ (not جَزَرَةً).",
     "Unvocalized عند can also be the verb عَنَدَ, “to be stubborn” (root of عِنَاد, stubbornness — Turkish inat)."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "عِنْدِي is for what you own or keep at your place, لِي for relations and rights, مَعِي for what you carry right now."
    ]
   }
